\description{
A \code{categorical} is a vector of codes in a \code{\link[=category_domain]{category_domain()}}. Codes \verb{1..size}
are the domain's categories and code 0 means undefined, which is allowed only
where the domain allows it. A categorical never holds \code{NA}.

\code{as_categorical()} makes one:
\itemize{
\item from a \strong{factor}, whose levels become the labels of a new domain, in
their order, unless \code{domain} is given. Name the level that stands for
code 0 with \code{undefined_level}. An ordered factor gives an ordered domain in
the order of its levels, so the undefined level keeps its place in that
order. \code{NA} is an error: give missing values a level of their own, such as
\code{"unknown"}, and name it as \code{undefined_level}.
\item from \strong{text}, matched against the labels of \code{domain}, which is required.
The domain says which categories exist; text cannot.
\item from \strong{whole numbers}, which are taken as codes in \code{domain}.
}

Text that names no category of the domain is an error rather than becoming
undefined, so that a mistyped category cannot vanish.

A categorical prints as its labels. It compares by equality with another in
the same domain, with text, or with numbers taken as codes. In an ordered
domain \code{<}, \code{<=}, \code{>} and \code{>=} compare by the domain's order, as do \code{\link[=sort]{sort()}}
and \code{\link[=order]{order()}}. Arithmetic is an error. \code{\link[=c]{c()}} combines categorical vectors in
the same domain. Assigning into a categorical accepts another in the same
domain or text.

\code{\%in\%} and \code{\link[=match]{match()}} compare labels, not codes, because R gives each side to
them separately. So \code{x \%in\% "act"} works, but numbers on the right are
compared with the labels, and a categorical from another domain is not
refused as it is by \code{==}.

\code{factor(x)} gives a factor with the levels that occur, as it does for any
vector, in the domain's order. To convert with every category of the domain
as a level, use \code{forcats::as_factor(x)}: it gives the levels in the domain's
order, with the undefined label first where the domain is unordered and
allows undefined, and an ordered factor for an ordered domain.

\code{domain()} returns the domain of a categorical, or the naxp of a
\code{naxp_encoded} vector, which logmu treats as a categorical whose domain is the
naxp.
